      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027d be happy to discuss more not in a comment chain. Feel free to start a chat with me tomorrow when you\u0027re available, but I\u0027ll respond for now with a comment since it\u0027s Sunday in your time.\n\n\u003e To truly work for Chrome as a replacement for git (as you note) it would need submodule support.\n\nI also thought this initially, but this turns out to not be true. jj\u0027s approach to submodules is to just ignore them entirely. So what we do is to add a [config](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:tools/jj/config.toml;l\u003d8-9;drc\u003d43ba919d47045a749ab259b45bccac0348c6d36b) to map `jj sync` to `tools/jj/sync.py`, which is basically the equivalent of:\n1) `git fetch origin main`\n2) `jj rebase -b mutable() -d main@origin` (similar to `git rebase-update`)\n3) `gclient sync`\n\nThis means that we use git to ensure that the submodules are up to date, rather than jj, so this turns out to be a non-issue for chromium. The UX could definitely be improved with submodule support, but right now, since we\u0027re using git\u0027s infra to do submodules, it\u0027s on par with git.\n\n\u003e Longer term, I expect it could become a supported way to interact with the source code\n\nI\u0027ve been performing analysis on this. We can categorize this into two areas - missing features for jj, and workflow support. AFAICT, workflow support comes down to the following:\n* Syncing changes (I wrote `jj sync` to solve this - [source](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:tools/jj/sync.py?q\u003djj%2Fsync.py\u0026ss\u003dchromium%2Fchromium%2Fsrc))\n* Uploading changes (I recently submitted a PR to `jj` which adds the `jj gerrit upload` command)\n* Running formatters (I\u0027ve already added [config](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:tools/jj/config.toml;l\u003d43-89;drc\u003d43ba919d47045a749ab259b45bccac0348c6d36b) for jj which should match depot_tools\u0027 formatters).\n* Running presubmits (I\u0027ve already added `jj upload`, which, roughly speaking, is equivalent to `jj fix \u0026\u0026 git cl presubmit \u0026\u0026 jj gerrit upload`)\n\nMissing jj features comes down to:\n* Gitattribute support ([github issue](https://github.com/jj-vcs/jj/issues/53)). This breaks line endings. We have a hack to work around it, but it needs to be done on every repo.\n* Submodule support could perhaps make the UX slightly nicer (eg. rebase local changes for submodules), but it\u0027s just as good as the git approach.\n\nI\u0027ve been using jj on chrome since I started on chrome in January, and I\u0027ve been working on adding features to it to get to feature parity with git. I\u0027ve created [go/jj-in-chromium](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1nFpVtcel4-8TWfbMbAU8W8FROmTjbRTv4-UrRqLRM_E/edit?tab\u003dt.0) and started [g/chromium-jjj-users](https://groups.google.com/g/chromium-jj-users), which currently has 27 members (we haven\u0027t yet sent out any emails about this, so this is just word of mouth).\n\n\u003e At that point yeah, should have things in depot_tools but ownership would probably transition to Source team\n\nMy thoughts were that while it was unofficially supported, I would maintain the jj support. Once it was officially supported, I would be happy to hand it off to the team that maintains depot_tools. Any project-specific configuration would be owned by that particular team.",
